---
import ToolPage from "../components/ToolPage.astro";
import SpecificActivity from "../components/calc/SpecificActivity.tsx";
import Faq from "../components/Faq.astro";
import { EX } from "../data/worked-examples";
const D = EX.specificActivity;
---
<ToolPage slug="specific-activity">
  <SpecificActivity slot="tool" client:only="react" />
  <Fragment slot="prose">
    <h2>Where the number comes from</h2>
    <p>
      Specific activity follows from two definitions and two measured quantities. A gram of a pure
      nuclide contains N<sub>A</sub>/M atoms, where M is the molar mass in grams per mole. Each atom
      decays with probability λ = ln 2 / T½ per second. Multiplying gives
      a = ln 2 · N<sub>A</sub> / (T½ · M) becquerel per gram, with nothing left to choose: no
      geometry, no efficiency, no convention. The two measured inputs are the half-life and the
      molar mass, and both are evaluated values with published uncertainties. The molar mass here is
      the evaluated atomic mass from AME2020, not the mass number — the two differ by the nuclear
      binding energy, which reaches 0.53% for tritium and changes sign across the chart of nuclides.
    </p>
    <p>
      The inverse relationship with half-life is the whole story. Tritium, with a half-life of 12.3
      years, has a specific activity of 3.6 × 10¹⁴ Bq/g. Uranium-238, at 4.5 billion years, has
      1.2 × 10⁴ Bq/g — ten orders of magnitude lower. A microgram of tritium is a serious source; a
      kilogram of depleted uranium is a paperweight that happens to be radioactive.
    </p>

    <h2>Why plutonium and uranium need care</h2>
    <p>
      The calculator returns the mass of the <em>pure isotope</em>. Real nuclear material never is
      one. Weapons-grade plutonium is mostly Pu-239 with several percent Pu-240 and smaller
      quantities of Pu-238, Pu-241 and Am-241 grown in from Pu-241 decay. Reactor-grade plutonium has
      far more of the heavier isotopes. Because Pu-238 and Pu-241 have specific activities thousands
      of times higher than Pu-239, a few percent of them dominates the total activity of the mixture
      even though they contribute almost nothing to its mass.
    </p>
    <p>
      The consequence for accountancy is that grams and becquerels are not interchangeable through a
      single number unless the isotopic vector is known. To convert a real sample, apply this
      calculation to each isotope with its own mass fraction and add the activities. The reverse
      direction — inferring a total mass from a measured total activity — requires the vector too,
      and is much more sensitive to it.
    </p>

    <h3>Americium ingrowth</h3>
    <p>
      Pu-241 beta-decays with a 14-year half-life to Am-241, which is a strong 59.5 keV photon
      emitter. Plutonium that was almost purely an alpha and low-energy problem when it was
      separated becomes a measurable gamma source over a couple of decades. Any mass-to-activity
      figure for aged plutonium that omits the americium is wrong in a way that grows with time.
    </p>

    <h2>Practical uses</h2>
    <p>
      Three come up constantly. Converting a waste inventory between the mass basis used by a
      chemical process and the activity basis used by a disposal limit. Checking whether a quoted
      source strength is physically possible for the stated mass — a claim that fails this check is
      usually a units error. And estimating the carrier mass in a tracer preparation, where the
      radioactive atoms may be a vanishing fraction of the material actually present.
    </p>
    <p>
      The last case is where intuition fails most often. A 37 MBq Tc-99m generator elution contains
      about two nanograms of Tc-99m. Everything else in the vial is carrier, saline and the long-lived
      Tc-99 ground state that the metastable state decays into.
    </p>

    <h2>Worked examples</h2>
    <h3>Checking a caesium-137 source certificate</h3>
    <p>
      A certificate describes a Cs-137 source of 37&nbsp;GBq (1&nbsp;Ci). How much caesium-137 is
      actually in it? Choose “Activity → mass” and Cs-137, enter 37&nbsp;GBq, and read the mass.
    </p>
    <div class="not-prose my-3 pin-first table-fit" tabindex="0">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Result</th><th class="text-right">Value</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Mass of Cs-137, mg</td><td class="num text-right">{D.cs1CiMg}</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Specific activity, Ci/g</td><td class="num text-right">{D.csCiPerG}</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      About {D.cs1CiMg}&nbsp;mg of Cs-137 makes one curie, so no more than about 87&nbsp;Ci can be
      packed into a gram of the pure nuclide. That gives a quick plausibility check in both directions.
      A document claiming 1&nbsp;Ci in a speck of 1&nbsp;mg has a units error somewhere. A real source
      capsule weighs far more than {D.cs1CiMg}&nbsp;mg because the caesium is a chloride or glass
      compound that also contains stable caesium isotopes from the fission product, so the source's own
      specific activity is lower than the nuclide's. The figure from this calculator is always the upper
      limit for the pure nuclide.
    </p>

    <h3>Why a gram of plutonium is not like a gram of uranium</h3>
    <p>
      Choose “Mass → activity” and enter 1&nbsp;g, first for U-238 and then for Pu-239. Then switch to
      “Activity → mass”, choose Am-241 and enter 37&nbsp;kBq, the typical activity in an ionisation
      smoke detector.
    </p>
    <div class="not-prose my-3 pin-first table-fit" tabindex="0">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Input</th><th class="text-right">Result</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>1 g of U-238, kBq</td><td class="num text-right">{D.u238KBq}</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1 g of Pu-239, GBq</td><td class="num text-right">{D.pu239GBq}</td></tr>
          <tr><td>37 kBq of Am-241, µg</td><td class="num text-right">{D.am37kBqUg}</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Gram for gram, Pu-239 is about {D.puURatio} times more active than U-238, because its half-life
      is about 185&nbsp;000 times shorter and the molar masses are almost equal. Only {D.puSameUg}&nbsp;µg
      of Pu-239 has the activity of a whole gram of U-238. The smoke-detector source is a third of a
      microgram of americium. Masses this small are why alpha emitters are controlled by activity and
      not by weight, and why a weighing balance tells you nothing useful about them. It works the other
      way too: a kilogram of depleted uranium shielding holds only about 12&nbsp;MBq of U-238, which is
      why its main hazard is chemical toxicity and the beta dose from its decay products at the surface,
      not the alpha activity of the uranium itself.
    </p>

    <Faq slug="specific-activity" />
  </Fragment>
</ToolPage>
